{"id": "q066", "question": "What does Naaman's story teach about pride?", "gold_verses": ["2Kgs.5.10", "2Kgs.5.11", "2Kgs.5.12", "2Kgs.5.13", "2Kgs.5.14"], "gold_commentary_chunks": ["MHC.2Kgs.5"], "category": "interpretive", "source": "LLM-generated candidate, verified against MHC on 2 Kgs 5 (Bible Hub)", "notes": "Verses give Naaman's anger and eventual healing. MHC develops the deeper point: Elisha deliberately uses a humbling instruction because he knows Naaman is proud, and MHC reads the whole episode as a picture of sinners refusing the gospel's simplicity — 'the way a sinner is received and made holy through faith alone does not sufficiently humour self'. 'The pride blocks grace' reading isn't in the verses, it's MHC's generalisation", "min_recall": 1, "excluded": false}
{"id": "q064", "question": "Why does Jesus praise the dishonest manager?", "gold_verses": ["Luke.16.1", "Luke.16.5", "Luke.16.6", "Luke.16.7", "Luke.16.8", "Luke.16.9"], "gold_commentary_chunks": ["MHC.Luke.16"], "category": "interpretive", "source": "LLM-generated candidate, verified against MHC on Luke 16 (Bible Hub)", "notes": "Verses give the parable but leave the reader puzzled as Jesus appears to praise dishonesty. MHC resolves this directly: the master commended the policy (strategic foresight), not the fraud. Christians should imitate the energy and forward-planning of worldly people in pursuing spiritual ends, not their methods. The fraud-vs-policy distinction is the load-bearing interpretive move, the verse alone leaves it ambiguous", "min_recall": 1, "excluded": false}
{"id": "q067", "question": "What's the meaning of the bronze serpent Moses lifted up?", "gold_verses": ["Num.21.6", "Num.21.7", "Num.21.8", "Num.21.9"], "gold_commentary_chunks": ["MHC.Num.21"], "category": "interpretive", "source": "LLM-generated candidate, verified against MHC on Num 21 (Bible Hub)", "notes": "Verses give a strange OT episode, namely, snakes, a bronze serpent on a pole, healing by looking. MHC reads the serpent as a picture of Christ on the cross, anchored on Jesus' own use of the image in John 3:14. The parallel is developed point by point: sin bites like the serpent, looking at the serpent in faith is looking to Christ in faith, whoever looks is healed however bad their case is. The Christ connection is the entire interpretive payload, the verses alone leave the reader with a puzzling story", "min_recall": 1, "excluded": false}

